Interesting — E31 and E32 show zero orphaned orders in the t=513–590 window; all messages there are "done". Yet E1 still reports 174 violations and E10 shows queue_depth at 406. This means the orphaned orders were created *after* the rollback (t=590), not during the crash window. The surge in orders_created (E6, peak 12.73/s at t=590) likely created a backlog of new orders whose messages haven't been picked up yet. I need to find where these stuck messages actually are.

```json
{
  "investigate": [
    {
      "tool": "sql",
      "query": "SELECT m.state, m.attempts, COUNT(*) FROM messages m JOIN orders o ON m.order_id = o.id WHERE o.created_at > 590 GROUP BY m.state, m.attempts ORDER BY m.state, m.attempts",
      "why": "Identify the state and attempt counts of messages for orders created after the rollback, to determine whether they are pending/stuck or being retried, and whether the queue is simply draining slowly or something is blocking processing."
    }
  ],
  "hypotheses": [
    {
      "service": "worker",
      "kind": "bad_deploy",
      "mechanism": "Worker v1.0.1 deployed at t=485 caused repeated health-check failures and restarts from t=513, leaving queue messages unprocessed and accumulating 174 orphaned orders; rollback to v1.0 at t=590 stopped the crashes but did not re-enqueue the stranded messages.",
      "supporting": ["E1", "E10", "E11", "E12", "E14", "E15"],
      "refuting": ["E31", "E32"],
      "explains": ["I2_no_orphaned_orders"],
      "repair_plan": [
        {"action": "requeue_orphaned_orders", "params": {}}
      ]
    }
  ],
  "summary": "Orphaned orders are not in the t=513-590 crash window; investigating post-rollback messages to find what is causing the remaining 174 violations and queue depth of 406."
}
```
